## The slot at the top

<At levels="simple">

Chat models have a slot at the top of the conversation for a <GlossaryPopover term="system-prompt">system prompt</GlossaryPopover>:
instructions for the whole chat. In a chat app, its makers write it and you never see it. Chat
models were trained to follow instructions like these, so they usually do.<Cite id="ouyang2022" />
But what kind of instructions help?

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The chat template (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />) has a slot ahead of the conversation for a <GlossaryPopover term="system-prompt">system prompt</GlossaryPopover>:
instructions that apply to the whole chat. In a chat app, its makers write it and you never see
it; when you use a model directly, you write it yourself. Chat models were tuned on examples of an
assistant following instructions, and on
people's ratings of which replies were better.<Cite id="ouyang2022" /> So instructions there tend
to be followed. But what kind of instructions help?

</At>

Here is Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct answering the same question under three system prompts. The
highlighted part is the only text that changes:

<SystemPrompts />

## Specific beats grand

<At levels="simple">

The specific instructions worked: two short sentences and an everyday example. The persona,
"a world-famous physicist", made the answer more than twice as long, and no better.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The specific instructions worked: two short sentences and an everyday example, shorter than the
default answer. The persona, "a world-famous physicist", made the answer more than twice
as long and no better: it added claims that are muddled, like most blue light being "scattered away
from us towards the edges of the sky".

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Telling the model it was "a brilliant mathematician who never makes mistakes" didn't help either:
it got 3 of 8 sums right, against 5 of 8 without it. A large study of 162 personas found the same:
on average, personas didn't make answers more accurate.<Cite id="zheng2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The sums tell the same story. Telling the model it is "a brilliant mathematician who never makes
mistakes" did not make it one: it got 3 of the 8 right, against 5 of 8 with the default system
prompt. A large study found the same across 162 personas and 2,410 factual questions: adding a
persona did not improve accuracy on average, and which persona helped on which question was largely
random.<Cite id="zheng2024" />

</At>

That fits how the model works. A persona changes which documents the text resembles, so it changes
tone, length, and vocabulary. It doesn't add knowledge or skill the model lacks. Instructions that
say what you actually want (who the reader is, how long, what format) give the model something it
can follow.

## Many rules at once

<At levels="simple">

Real system prompts often hold dozens of rules. Here Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct wrote 12 short notes,
each under 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, and then 10 rules that a program can check, like "Don't use any
commas":

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

System prompts in real apps often hold dozens of rules. Here Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct wrote 12 short
notes, each under 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, and then 10 rules, all of which can be kept at once and checked by
a program: start with "hello", use no commas, end with "see you soon", and so on. Each note starts
the list at a different rule, so every rule comes both early and late:

</At>

<RuleCount />

<At levels="simple">

More rules didn't make it worse at each one: with ten rules it kept 88 of 120, about three in four.
But every rule it misses spoils the note. With one rule, 7 of the 12 notes kept it; with ten, not
one note kept them all. Much larger models slip too as rules pile up: the best of 20 kept only about
two thirds of 500 rules.<Cite id="jaroslawicz2025" /> So check every rule that matters, and split a
long list into steps (<ChapterRef to="splitting-the-work" />).

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

More rules didn't make the model worse at each one: with a single rule it kept it in 7 of the 12
notes, and with ten it kept 88 of 120, about three in four. What fell is the chance that a note
kept *every* rule: 7 of 12 with one rule, 3 with four, 1 with eight, and none with ten. The misses
were mostly in rules that fight the model's habits, like using no "the" or adding a question, and
they came from all over the list. A benchmark of 500 rules found that much larger models slip too
as rules pile up: the best of 20 kept only 68% at the full 500, and models favored rules near the
start of the list.<Cite id="jaroslawicz2025" /> So a long list of rules needs checking, one rule at a
time, and it often works better split into steps (<ChapterRef to="splitting-the-work" />).

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Why "all of them" falls so fast

If each rule is kept with chance $p$, and the rules are kept or missed independently, all $k$ are
kept with chance

$$
p^k
$$

With $p = 88/120 \approx 0.73$, ten rules give $0.73^{10} \approx 4\%$: about half a note in 12, and
the recording got none. A rule that's kept 90% of the time still leaves only $0.9^{10} \approx 35\%$
of outputs with all ten right.

</At>

<At levels="deep">

### One sequence, ranked by training

In the template from <ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />, the system prompt sits between
`<|im_start|>system` and `<|im_end|>`, and the user's message follows in the same way. Leave the
system prompt out and Qwen's template fills one in: "You are Qwen, created by Alibaba Cloud. You
are a helpful assistant." Either way, the network gets one sequence of tokens. Nothing in its
layers treats the system part differently; any extra weight it gets was learned in tuning.

That's a weakness when other text in the sequence gives orders. OpenAI researchers argued that
treating system prompts and untrusted text as equals is one of the main things that lets prompt
injection work (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />). They trained GPT-3.5 on generated examples
of an *instruction hierarchy*, with system messages ranked above users' and users' above tool
outputs, teaching it to ignore lower-ranked instructions that conflict. It became much harder to
attack, even in ways it hadn't been trained on, while losing little on ordinary
tasks.<Cite id="wallace2024" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- The system prompt is text in a slot at the start of the chat, read like any other text.
- Clear, specific instructions about audience, length, and format are followed well.
- Each extra rule is another chance to miss one: with ten, no note kept them all.
- A persona changes style, not ability; "you never make mistakes" doesn't make it so.

<At levels="deep">

- Roles are marked by special tokens in one sequence; any priority for the system prompt comes
  from tuning.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** what about asking the model to work step by step, or to answer in a fixed format?
